tongxin-136:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Use either a uniqueness constraint or one inequality per unordered pair. The legal domain must contain at least as many values as the array.

| Array | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 0, 2, 5, 7 | Legal |
| 0, 2, 2, 7 | Illegal: repeated 2 |

### Explanation
The two classes below describe the same legal set: four distinct values chosen from zero through seven. The second formulation checks only i < j, avoiding self-comparisons and duplicate pair checks. The element type alone is not a useful domain specification: an unbounded int permits negative values and needlessly enlarges the solver problem. Check randomize() at the call site before consuming an object.

### Code
```systemverilog
class unique_array;
  rand bit [2:0] a[4];
  constraint distinct_c { unique {a}; }
endclass
class pairwise_array;
  rand bit [2:0] a[4];
  constraint distinct_c {
    foreach (a[i]) foreach (a[j]) if (i < j) a[i] != a[j];
  }
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Four elements with a domain of only three values are unsatisfiable. Unique does not imply sorted order or uniform sampling of every possible array. Pairwise constraints require N(N-1)/2 comparisons; this becomes expensive for large N. Do not include a[i] != a[i].

### Follow-ups
For a permutation of 0..N-1, fill the array with those values and shuffle if procedural generation is permitted; this avoids solving pairwise inequalities.

tongxin-137:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Constrain the command to a non-random next-command state, then advance that state only after successful randomization.

| Successful call | cmd | next_cmd afterward |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | READ | WRITE |
| 2 | WRITE | READ |
| 3 | READ | WRITE |

### Explanation
post_randomize executes after a successful solve. It should update the state for the next transaction rather than overwrite the command just solved. That preserves constraints involving the current command and payload. This example alternates within one reused generator object; constructing a new object each time intentionally restarts at READ.

### Code
```systemverilog
typedef enum bit {READ, WRITE} command_t;
class alternating_item;
  rand command_t cmd;
  rand bit [7:0] data;
  command_t next_cmd = READ;
  constraint cmd_c { cmd == next_cmd; }
  constraint payload_c { if (cmd == READ) data == 0; }
  function void post_randomize();
    next_cmd = (next_cmd == READ) ? WRITE : READ;
  endfunction
  function void reset_order(); next_cmd = READ; endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
An inline cmd == WRITE constraint on the first call fails; it cannot override the hard READ requirement. The failed solve must not consume an alternation step. If command order refers to accepted bus transactions, randomization is the wrong event to advance state: a generated item might be discarded or retried.

### Follow-ups
For multiple producers, centralize accepted-command ordering in a sequencer or shared generator rather than relying on independent per-object state.

tongxin-140:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Exclude every circular rotation of a supplied reference; an ordinary logical shift is not a rotation.

| Reference | Rotation | Status for candidate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 10000001 | 10000001 | Reject if equality forbidden |
| 10000001 | 11000000 | Reject |
| 10000001 | 00000101 | Allowed: not a rotation |

### Explanation
The prompt does not identify a reference, so this implementation accepts one through the constructor. It precomputes all eight forbidden 8-bit patterns. The non-random forbidden array becomes state for the solver. Rotation zero excludes equality; start the constraint loop at one instead if equality is allowed, while remembering that a symmetric reference may rotate back to itself.

### Code
```systemverilog
class no_rotation;
  rand bit [7:0] v;
  bit [7:0] forbidden[8];
  function new(bit [7:0] reference);
    bit [7:0] r = reference;
    for (int k = 0; k < 8; k++) begin
      forbidden[k] = r;
      r = {r[0], r[7:1]};
    end
  endfunction
  constraint avoid_c {
    foreach (forbidden[k]) v != forbidden[k];
  }
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
00000000 has just one distinct rotation, while 01010101 has two. Excluding eight entries therefore does not always exclude eight distinct values. If every pair in an array must be rotation-distinct, this single-reference solution must be extended to compare all unordered pairs.

### Follow-ups
For repeated generation, preserve previous rotation classes explicitly; otherwise a later call may legally repeat an earlier result.

tongxin-141:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Exactly four ones in a four-bit vector means the only legal result is 4'b1111.

| Vector | Number of ones | Legal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1111 | 4 | Yes |
| 1110 | 3 | No |
| 0000 | 0 | No |

### Explanation
No counting operator is necessary for the stated width. A constant equality is the simplest complete constraint. Although randomize succeeds, there is no entropy in this field. Other unconstrained rand fields in the same object can still vary. If the intended question was “four ones in a wider vector,” confirm the width before implementing that different problem.

### Code
```systemverilog
class four_ones;
  rand bit [3:0] v;
  constraint all_set_c { v == 4'b1111; }
endclass
// Different problem: exactly four ones in an 8-bit vector.
class four_of_eight;
  rand bit [7:0] v;
  constraint population_c {
    int'(v[0]) + int'(v[1]) + int'(v[2]) + int'(v[3]) +
    int'(v[4]) + int'(v[5]) + int'(v[6]) + int'(v[7]) == 4;
  }
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
The int casts in the wider example make the intended arithmetic width explicit. Do not use one-bit arithmetic for a population sum or claim that randomization produces multiple four-bit answers. A four-state input would additionally require an X/Z policy; the rand field here is two-state.

### Follow-ups
The wider eight-bit version has C(8,4)=70 legal bit patterns; the four-bit version has C(4,4)=1.

tongxin-142:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** shuffle preserves values and their multiplicities, but it does not preserve constraints tied to array positions.

| Stage | Example array |
| --- | --- |
| After solve | 1,1,2,2 |
| After shuffle | 2,1,2,1 |

### Explanation
This example constructs a multiset containing two ones and two twos. The indexed constraints describe an intermediate arrangement only; post_randomize deliberately destroys that arrangement while preserving the desired final counts. That distinction must be part of the contract. If positions themselves are requirements, do not shuffle after solving.

### Code
```systemverilog
class shuffled_multiset;
  rand int a[4];
  constraint build_c {
    foreach (a[i]) {
      if (i < 2) a[i] == 1;
      else       a[i] == 2;
    }
  }
  function void post_randomize(); a.shuffle(); endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
The returned array will generally no longer satisfy build_c. Inline constraints on final positions can likewise be broken after the solver succeeds. Sum, minimum, maximum, uniqueness, and counts survive a permutation; sortedness, adjacency, and index-dependent bounds do not. Check randomize failure before use; post_randomize is not a replacement for error handling.

### Follow-ups
To keep constraints true on the final object, specify counts directly with array reductions, for example a.sum() with (int'(item == 1)) == 2, and constrain the alphabet to {1,2}.

tongxin-144:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Named constraint replacement, constraint_mode, and soft defaults solve different override needs.

| Mechanism | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| Same block name in child | Replaces inherited block of that name |
| constraint_mode(0) | Disables selected hard block |
| soft constraint | Default can yield to conflicting hard constraint |

### Explanation
Use same-name replacement when the derived type defines a new policy. Use constraint_mode when the test chooses which policy is active at runtime. Use soft for a genuine default that callers are allowed to supersede. An inline hard constraint is conjoined with active hard class constraints; it does not automatically override them.

### Code
```systemverilog
class base_item;
  rand int x;
  constraint range_c { x inside {[0:9]}; }
endclass
class child_item extends base_item;
  constraint range_c { x inside {[20:29]}; }
endclass
class default_item;
  rand int x;
  constraint default_c { soft x == 5; }
endclass
module example;
  initial begin
    base_item b = new;
    default_item d = new;
    b.range_c.constraint_mode(0);
    if (!b.randomize() with { x == 25; }) $fatal(1, "solve failed");
    if (!d.randomize() with { x == 8; }) $fatal(1, "solve failed");
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Disabling a block removes all constraints in that block, including bounds you may still need. Name replacement does not disable unrelated inherited blocks. Document which fields have defaults and which encode immutable legality.

### Follow-ups
If inline x==25 fails against an active x inside {[0:9]}, inspect active hard constraints instead of trying solve-before; solve order cannot make contradictory constraints satisfiable.

tongxin-146:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Measure event-to-event elapsed time; a reference-clock assertion can miss an entire narrow pulse.

| Rise | Fall | High width | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 10 ns | 12.4 ns | 2.4 ns | Fail |
| 20 ns | 22.5 ns | 2.5 ns | Pass |

### Explanation
The checker measures known-high intervals using realtime and a 1 ps simulation precision. A falling transition ends the interval and triggers an immediate assertion. Reset aborts the current measurement. If reset is released while the signal is high, this implementation starts measuring at release, so it checks only the observed post-reset part of that pulse. This startup policy is explicit rather than pretending the unknown earlier rise time is available.

### Code
```systemverilog
module min_high_checker(input logic sig, rst_n);
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  realtime rise_at;
  bit have_rise = 0;
  // One process owns pulse state; X/Z invalidate the measurement.
  always @(sig or rst_n) begin
    if (rst_n !== 1'b1 || $isunknown(sig)) have_rise = 0;
    else if (sig === 1'b1) begin
      rise_at = $realtime;
      have_rise = 1;
    end else if (have_rise) begin
      assert (($realtime - rise_at) >= 2.5ns)
        else $error("High pulse too short: %0t", $realtime-rise_at);
      have_rise = 0;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
X/Z invalidates pulse tracking; add an independent known-value assertion if unknowns must be errors. The checker sees transitions represented in the simulator, not analog excursions or changes rounded away by timeprecision. It checks high pulses only and does not report a signal stuck high, because no falling edge arrives.

### Follow-ups
For a minimum low width, mirror the timestamp logic. For maximum high width or a stopped signal, add a separately scheduled watchdog with cancellation for completed pulses.

