tongxin-147:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Once valid is sampled high during a stall, it must still be high at the next sample, including the eventual accepting sample.

```wavedrom
{
  "signal": [
    {
      "name": "clk",
      "wave": "p....."
    },
    {
      "name": "valid",
      "wave": "01..0."
    },
    {
      "name": "ready",
      "wave": "0..10."
    }
  ]
}
```

### Explanation
A transfer occurs when valid and ready are both sampled high. Each stalled cycle creates a one-cycle obligation, so an arbitrarily long stall becomes a chain of checks. The payload check ensures the receiver sees the same item when it eventually accepts. No obligation to keep valid high is generated by an accepting cycle; the producer may remove it on the next cycle or keep it high for the next item.

### Code
```systemverilog
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  valid && !ready |=> valid && $stable(payload));
cover property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  (valid && !ready)[*3] ##1 (valid && ready));
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
This safety property intentionally allows ready to remain low forever. Add bounded progress only if the interface contract guarantees it, and assign responsibility to the receiver. If only valid persistence is specified, omit $stable(payload). Unknown controls deserve explicit checks rather than relying on implication to detect every X.

### Follow-ups
For a skid buffer, check the same contract independently on input and output and compare only accepted transactions through a scoreboard.

tongxin-151:
> Completed by Codex.
> Reviewed and corrected by Claude.

**Key idea:** Check APB setup, access, wait-state stability, and exit independently; PSEL may remain asserted for back-to-back transfers.

```wavedrom
{
  "signal": [
    {
      "name": "PCLK",
      "wave": "p....."
    },
    {
      "name": "PSEL",
      "wave": "01..0."
    },
    {
      "name": "PENABLE",
      "wave": "0.1.0."
    },
    {
      "name": "PREADY",
      "wave": "0..10."
    }
  ]
}
```

Samples 1–3 are setup, wait-state access, and completing access; PSEL drops with PENABLE afterwards (keeping PSEL high with PENABLE low would start a new setup phase, which the first assertion then requires to enter access).

### Explanation
On the selected slave interface, setup is PSEL && !PENABLE and access is PSEL && PENABLE. PREADY is meaningful for transfer completion during access. The next sample after setup must enter access. Every waiting access sample must preserve the address, direction, and write data into the next sample. After a completing access, PENABLE must be low for a new setup or idle cycle. These properties assume an APB subset with no PSTRB or PPROT signals.

### Code
```systemverilog
assert property (@(posedge PCLK) disable iff (!PRESETn)
  PSEL && !PENABLE |=> PSEL && PENABLE &&
  $stable({PADDR,PWRITE}) && (!PWRITE || $stable(PWDATA)));
assert property (@(posedge PCLK) disable iff (!PRESETn)
  PSEL && PENABLE && !PREADY |=>
  PSEL && PENABLE && $stable({PADDR,PWRITE}) &&
  (!PWRITE || $stable(PWDATA)));
assert property (@(posedge PCLK) disable iff (!PRESETn)
  PSEL && PENABLE && PREADY |=> !PENABLE);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Setup-to-access and waiting-access stability are both checked above. PENABLE may be high while this particular slave is unselected in a multi-slave system, so do not blindly assert PENABLE implies this slave's PSEL. Add version-specific control stability and known-value checks.

### Follow-ups
Does APB require eventual PREADY? Not inherently within a fixed bound; add a timeout only from the system-level requirement.

tongxin-159:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** A four-phase req/gnt handshake follows 00→10→11→01→00, with each phase permitted to hold.

| Current pair | Allowed next pairs |
| --- | --- |
| 00 | 00 or 10 |
| 10 | 10 or 11 |
| 11 | 11 or 01 |
| 01 | 01 or 00 |

### Explanation
This explicit contract disallows skipping phases or changing both signals in one sampled cycle. It permits unbounded waiting in any phase, so a separate timeout is needed if progress is guaranteed. The SVA checks the next sampled pair based on the current pair. The procedural version stores the previous sampled pair and checks the same transition relation. Reset establishes the initial 00 phase; unknown values must be rejected.

### Code
```systemverilog
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  {req,gnt} == 2'b00 |=> !gnt);
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  {req,gnt} == 2'b10 |=> req);
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  {req,gnt} == 2'b11 |=> gnt);
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  {req,gnt} == 2'b01 |=> !req);
logic [1:0] previous = 0;
always @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n) begin
  if (!rst_n) previous = 0;
  else begin
    assert (!$isunknown({req,gnt}));
    case (previous)
      2'b00: assert (!gnt);
      2'b10: assert (req);
      2'b11: assert (gnt);
      2'b01: assert (!req);
    endcase
    previous = {req,gnt};
  end
end
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
The procedural checker also constrains the first post-reset sample using previous=00; add the corresponding startup check if exact SVA equivalence is required. Drive signals away from the sampling edge to avoid races. No req can pass every implication vacuously.

### Follow-ups
If zero-latency grant or same-cycle cleanup is legal, widen the transition table first, then update both implementations consistently.

tongxin-162:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Functions compute without advancing simulation time; tasks may perform timed activity.

| Use | Construct |
| --- | --- |
| Checksum / comparison | function |
| Bus transfer / wait for reset | task |
| Return no value without timing | function void |

### Explanation
Choose a function when the result must be available within an expression or a zero-time callback. Choose a task for stimulus that waits for clocks, delays, or handshakes. Both support arguments and automatic lifetime. A function may contain computation and zero-time system calls, but it cannot call an ordinary time-consuming task or contain timing controls. A task can call a function.

### Code
```systemverilog
function automatic bit [7:0] checksum(input bit [7:0] a, b);
  return a ^ b;
endfunction
task automatic send_byte(input bit [7:0] value);
  @(negedge clk);
  data = value;
  valid = 1;
  do @(posedge clk); while (!ready);
  @(negedge clk);
  valid = 0;
endtask
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
The task assumes data/valid are testbench-driven and ready is stable for sampling; a clocking block is preferable for a reusable driver. Static local storage can create reentrancy bugs when concurrent callers share temporaries. Declaring automatic isolates invocation-local variables, not referenced shared objects.

### Follow-ups
Why are UVM build_phase and write callbacks functions? Their contracts require zero-time behavior; run_phase is a task because it drives timed activity.

tongxin-165:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** A packed multidimensional array is already contiguous bits; streaming is needed when you want a particular rearrangement.

| Expression for 32'h11223344 | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Direct packed assignment | 11223344 |
| {>>{a}} | 11223344 |
| {<<8{a}} | 44332211 |

### Explanation
With declaration logic [3:0][7:0] a, the leftmost packed index is the most significant byte. Assigning a to a 32-bit vector preserves that packed ordering. Leftward streaming in 8-bit slices reverses the order of bytes while preserving the bit order inside each byte. Leftward streaming without a slice size reverses individual bits, which is a different operation.

### Code
```systemverilog
module packing;
  logic [3:0][7:0] a;
  logic [31:0] flat, reversed_bytes;
  initial begin
    a = 32'h11223344;
    flat = a;
    reversed_bytes = {<<8{a}};
    assert (flat == 32'h11223344);
    assert (reversed_bytes == 32'h44332211);
    assert (a[3] == 8'h11);
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Index direction matters: changing [3:0] to [0:3] changes which indexed element occupies the most significant slice. Unpacked arrays are not interchangeable with packed vectors through ordinary assignment in the same way. Document bus byte order rather than assuming host endianness.

### Follow-ups
To serialize an unpacked byte array, use a streaming expression or an explicit loop, then test with asymmetric bytes so reversals cannot accidentally pass.

tongxin-169:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** A class downcast must verify the object's runtime type; a numeric static cast only converts representation.

| Operation | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| int'(small) | Convert numeric type/width |
| $cast(child, parent) | Check class compatibility at runtime |

### Explanation
A base handle may point to either a base object or a derived object. Assignment from derived to base is naturally safe, but recovering a derived handle requires proof that the actual object supports the derived members. Use the function form of $cast to handle failure explicitly. A failed cast leaves the destination unchanged, so reusing an old destination handle without checking success can accidentally access an unrelated earlier object.

### Code
```systemverilog
class base_item; endclass
class data_item extends base_item;
  int payload;
endclass
module casting;
  initial begin
    base_item b;
    data_item d = new;
    data_item recovered;
    d.payload = 42;
    b = d;
    if (!$cast(recovered, b)) $fatal(1, "Wrong item type");
    else $display("payload=%0d", recovered.payload);
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
A successful cast does not clone the object; recovered and b refer to the same object. Guard null before dereferencing when null is possible. Do not substitute an unchecked type conversion for dynamic class compatibility checking.

### Follow-ups
If code repeatedly downcasts merely to invoke behavior, put a virtual method on the base class and let polymorphism select the implementation.

tongxin-172:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** An abstract class defines an uninstantiable interface; a singleton restricts a concrete class to one accessible instance.

| Pattern | Construction | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| virtual class | Cannot instantiate directly | Require implementations |
| Singleton | Via static accessor | Share one instance |

### Explanation
A pure virtual method declares behavior without implementation. Any concrete derived class must implement that contract before it can be instantiated. The singleton pattern instead restricts construction and caches one handle. These patterns are orthogonal: an abstract interface can be implemented by a concrete singleton, but an abstract class does not imply a unique instance of its subclasses.

### Code
```systemverilog
virtual class encoder;
  pure virtual function bit [7:0] encode(bit [7:0] value);
endclass
class identity_encoder extends encoder;
  virtual function bit [7:0] encode(bit [7:0] value);
    return value;
  endfunction
endclass
class settings;
  local static settings instance;
  local function new(); endfunction
  static function settings get();
    if (instance == null) instance = new;
    return instance;
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
A protected constructor permits derived classes to call it; a local constructor is stricter. Static state persists across test activity, so do not mistake DUT reset for singleton reinitialization. Multiple handles returned by get refer to the same object, not equivalent copies.

### Follow-ups
Why use an abstract class in verification? A scoreboard can depend on a predictor interface while tests choose different concrete prediction models without changing scoreboard logic.

tongxin-173:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Passing a handle by value shares the object; passing it by ref also shares the caller's handle variable.

| Callee operation | input handle | ref handle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| h.x = 7 | Caller object changes | Caller object changes |
| h = new | Caller handle unchanged | Caller handle replaced |

### Explanation
A class variable stores a reference to an object. Copying that variable creates another reference to the same object, so field mutation is visible through both handles. Reassigning a copied input handle changes only the callee's copy. A ref formal aliases the actual handle variable, enabling the callee to replace the caller's reference. It does not automatically deep-copy or protect the old object.

### Code
```systemverilog
class item; int x; endclass
function automatic void by_value(input item h);
  h.x = 7;
  h = new;
  h.x = 99;
endfunction
function automatic void by_ref(ref item h);
  h = new;
  h.x = 42;
endfunction
module handles;
  initial begin
    item a = new;
    by_value(a); assert (a.x == 7);
    by_ref(a);   assert (a.x == 42);
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Dereferencing a null input handle is invalid. ref arguments require compatible actual variable types and automatic subroutine lifetime. A const ref handle prevents assigning that handle variable; it is not a general deep immutability guarantee for the referenced object graph.

### Follow-ups
To isolate a scoreboard from later producer mutation, explicitly copy/clone the object and any nested mutable state, rather than changing argument direction.

tongxin-176:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Sample only meaningful accepted values, and create bins that answer the verification plan's questions.

| Bin | Values | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| zero | 0 | Lower boundary |
| middle[4] | 1..254 partitioned | Range distribution |
| maximum | 255 | Upper boundary |
| wrap | 255=>0 | Transition scenario |

### Explanation
An 8-bit field permits 256 values, but one bin per value is useful only if every encoding matters. Range and boundary bins may give a clearer plan. The sample method below explicitly receives the observed byte and mode, so a monitor can invoke it only when a transaction is accepted. Cross coverage shows whether each data region appeared in both modes.

### Code
```systemverilog
covergroup byte_coverage with function sample(bit [7:0] value, bit mode);
  option.per_instance = 1;
  cp_data: coverpoint value {
    bins zero = {0};
    bins middle[4] = {[1:254]};
    bins maximum = {255};
    bins wrap = (255 => 0);
  }
  cp_mode: coverpoint mode;
  data_by_mode: cross cp_data, cp_mode;
endgroup
// In a monitor: if (rst_n && valid && ready) cg.sample(data, mode);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
The code uses two-state sampling arguments; check for X/Z before calling sample if unknown bus data is an error. Transition bins relate successive sample calls, not necessarily adjacent clock cycles. Coverage says an event occurred, not that its result was correct. Use assertions or a scoreboard for correctness.

### Follow-ups
If every encoding is required, replace the value bins with bins each_value[] = {[0:255]}; review cross size before adding several other dimensions.

tongxin-177:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Use an interface to define AHB-Lite connectivity and clocking blocks to state exactly when the testbench samples and drives.

| Accepted edge | Address phase | Data phase |
| --- | --- | --- |
| E0 | Transfer A | Previous transfer |
| E1 | Transfer B | Data/response A |
| E2 | Transfer C | Data/response B |

### Explanation
The interface below is a minimal 32-bit AHB-Lite signal set. input #1step samples just before the edge, avoiding confusion with DUT nonblocking updates at that edge. Driver outputs have zero skew, providing the next cycle's values after the sampled edge. AHB address and data phases overlap, so a driver must retain the preceding accepted transfer's direction and data separately from the current address phase.

### Code
```systemverilog
interface ahb_if(input logic HCLK, HRESETn);
  logic [31:0] HADDR, HWDATA, HRDATA;
  logic [1:0] HTRANS;
  logic [2:0] HSIZE, HBURST;
  logic HWRITE, HREADY, HRESP;
  clocking drv_cb @(posedge HCLK);
    default input #1step output #0;
    input HRESETn, HREADY, HRESP, HRDATA;
    output HADDR, HTRANS, HSIZE, HBURST, HWRITE, HWDATA;
  endclocking
  clocking mon_cb @(posedge HCLK);
    default input #1step;
    input HRESETn, HADDR, HTRANS, HSIZE, HBURST, HWRITE;
    input HWDATA, HREADY, HRESP, HRDATA;
  endclocking
  modport DRIVER(clocking drv_cb);
  modport MONITOR(clocking mon_cb);
endinterface
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
This is an interface skeleton, not a complete AHB interconnect or driver. Add HPROT, HMASTLOCK, and slave-selection signals as needed. Advance address acceptance only when HREADY && HTRANS[1]; keep control stable during stalls and retain write data for the stalled data phase.

### Follow-ups
Why not use one transaction per clock? Wait states and pipelining decouple address acceptance from later data completion, so the monitor must track both phases.

tongxin-180:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Have each worker send a completion token; consume two tokens before calling the function.

| Completion order | After first token | After second token |
| --- | --- | --- |
| B, A, C | Wait | Call function after A |
| A and C together, then B | Consume one | Consume second; call once |

### Explanation
A typed unbounded mailbox counts completions without a racy shared read-modify-write increment. Even if two workers finish at the same simulation time, they enqueue separate tokens and the parent receives both. The example retains the third worker and waits for it after invoking the function. If cancellation is required instead, use a process-isolated fork and explicit cleanup policy.

### Code
```systemverilog
module two_of_three;
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  mailbox #(int) completed = new;
  task automatic worker(int id, time delay_time);
    #(delay_time);
    completed.put(id);
  endtask
  function void after_two(); $display("Two completed"); endfunction
  initial begin
    int id;
    fork
      worker(0, 3ns);
      worker(1, 1ns);
      worker(2, 5ns);
    join_none
    repeat (2) completed.get(id);
    after_two();
    wait fork;
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Each worker must publish exactly one token after its actual work finishes. For repeated rounds, use a fresh mailbox or drain all tokens; the third token from the previous round must not count as a new completion. The callback function cannot consume simulation time.

### Follow-ups
If individual identities matter, track received IDs and reject duplicates; a token count alone assumes each worker follows the publication contract.

sva-scen-003:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** Use $onehot for exactly one grant, or $onehot0 when idle with no grant is legal, and reject unknowns separately.

| Sample / case | Expected behavior |
| --- | --- |
| 00001 or 10000 | Exactly one: pass |
| 00110 | Two grants: fail |
| 00000 | Pass only for onehot0 |

### Explanation
The wording can mean exactly one active grant or at most one. Arbitration usually allows idle, so the choice must be explicit. $onehot counts known one bits; combining it with !$isunknown prevents a vector containing X/Z from being treated as a valid grant mask. The vector is five bits wide in this example. All properties use rising-edge sampling on clk. Unless stated otherwise, rst_n is an active-low reset and disable iff (!rst_n) aborts pending attempts during reset. Controls are assumed known 0/1; add explicit unknown checks when X/Z must be reported.

### Code
```systemverilog
logic [4:0] grant;
// Exactly one:
assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  !$isunknown(grant) && $onehot(grant));
// Alternative when no winner is legal:
// assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
//   !$isunknown(grant) && $onehot0(grant));
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
One-hotness does not establish that a granted requester actually requested service or that arbitration is fair. Check grant & ~request == 0 separately when requests and grants refer to the same sampled cycle. Pipelines may require delayed request correspondence.

### Follow-ups
To verify fairness, add a bounded-wait property under stated resource-availability assumptions; one-hot checks alone cannot detect starvation.

sva-scen-018:
> Completed by Codex.

**Key idea:** A glitch checker needs a duration or legal-transition rule; this example rejects known-high pulses shorter than 2.5 ns.

| Pulse width | Result |
| --- | --- |
| 2.4 ns | Glitch |
| 2.5 ns | Allowed |
| Pulse between reference edges | Still detected by signal events |

### Explanation
The prompt gives no numerical threshold, so 2.5 ns is an explicit illustrative choice. Event timestamps detect pulses that a clocked property can miss completely. This checker measures only observed high intervals and clears history during reset or X/Z. If reset releases while the signal is high, the observed interval begins at release. That conservative policy should be changed if startup-high pulses are exempt.

### Code
```systemverilog
module min_high_checker(input logic sig, rst_n);
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  realtime rise_at;
  bit have_rise = 0;
  // One process owns pulse state; X/Z invalidate the measurement.
  always @(sig or rst_n) begin
    if (rst_n !== 1'b1 || $isunknown(sig)) have_rise = 0;
    else if (sig === 1'b1) begin
      rise_at = $realtime;
      have_rise = 1;
    end else if (have_rise) begin
      assert (($realtime - rise_at) >= 2.5ns)
        else $error("High pulse too short: %0t", $realtime-rise_at);
      have_rise = 0;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
Digital simulation cannot detect analog excursions absent from the signal model, and timeprecision limits representable pulse widths. An X/Z transition cancels this measurement; report unknowns separately if they are illegal. A signal stuck high never produces a falling edge, so a maximum-duration watchdog is a separate requirement.

### Follow-ups
To detect forbidden transitions outside a sampling aperture, define the legal aperture around the reference clock and timestamp every signal change; that is different from minimum pulse-width checking.
